  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with \n\n$$\n|4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x|\\le 4.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator ${x}^{2}$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ Since the denominator grows much faster than the (bounded) numerator, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{{x}^{2}},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13951126/assets/fd6dbbe8be077ed2eda955802f112540aa8aea73c5c65bae4f0f034176bbdccd.png)",
